aining target-radius sampling from 14-20 cm to the official 6-20 cm range will reduce the best-known policy's repeatable lower-left reach failures, because the missing near-target states are part of the learned reach policy's coverage gap.", "hypothesis_assessment": "The proposal expected fewer lower-left failures, especially in the newly covered 6-14 cm band, with a possible increase toward or above 98%. That expectation was contradicted: failures persisted in both the newly covered and previously covered radial bands, with no overall success improvement. The exact intervention therefore failed to remove the gap under this transfer run and budget. The broader causal mechanism remains unresolved: this result weakens radial coverage as the sole explanation but does not rule out other radial training designs or distinguish angular asymmetry, control/dynamics limits, representation, and stochastic effects.", "index": 2, "initialization": "transfer", "kind": "training", "paired_comparisons": [], "parameter_changes": [], "postmortem": "research/postmortems.md", "proposal_snapshot": {"change": "Change TRAINING_TARGET_RADIUS_RANGE in robot_learning/scenario/environment.py from (0.14, 0.20) to (0.06, 0.20), preserving full angular sampling and the official evaluation environment.", "family": "training.target_radius_coverage", "hypothesis": "Expanding training target-radius sampling from 14-20 cm to the official 6-20 cm range will reduce the best-known policy's repeatable lower-left reach failures, because the missing near-target states are part of the learned reach policy's coverage gap.", "initialization": "transfer", "kind": "training", "reasoning": {"alternative": "The failures are caused by an angularly asymmetric reach/control or dynamics limitation rather than radial coverage; under this explanation, adding near-target radii will not remove the repeated lower-left failures, including failures at radii already covered by the baseline.", "contradicting_observation": "If the same lower-left episodes remain failures across both the newly covered 6-14 cm band and the already covered 14-20 cm band, especially with no improvement in first reach or hold duration, radial coverage is weakened and angular/control asymmetry becomes the preferred explanation. A new increase in positive-angle hold failures would also show a stability tradeoff rather than a clean resolution.", "evidence": [{"observation": "The current strategy identifies checkpoint-100352 as 97.0% on both 200-episode panels, with six repeatable failures concentrated at target angles about -122 to -145 degrees, and states that the baseline trained only on 14-20 cm radii.", "source": "research/postmortems.md"}, {"observation": "The six failed episodes include targets at 6.00, 8.37, and 12.99 cm as well as 15.09, 16.07, and 16.63 cm; five never reached tolerance and the remaining target held for only one step.", "source": "research/evaluations/e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2/evaluation-e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2-experiment-1-checkpoint-100352-200ep-seed0-d72ec900e7de.json"}, {"observation": "The training environment currently samples radii from (0.14, 0.20), while make_evaluation_env uses the protected official TARGET_RADIUS_RANGE; this intervention changes only the researcher-owned training range to (0.06, 0.20).", "source": "robot_learning/scenario/environment.py"}, {"observation": "The official task samples targets uniformly from 6-20 cm over the full angular range and requires a continuous 2-second hold, so the intervention preserves the official angular support and evaluation semantics.", "source": "research/scenario.md"}], "expected_observation": "On the same compatible research panel, failures at the newly covered 6-14 cm radii should decrease, with the repeated lower-left reach failures showing fewer no-reach outcomes and a higher overall success rate toward or above 98%. Improvement concentrated in the newly covered radial band supports the radial-coverage explanation; broader lower-left improvement would indicate that near-target training also improves the shared angular policy.", "initialization_reason": "Use transfer from best_known because the intervention changes only target sampling; the observation construction, action mapping, robot dynamics, reward, policy dimensions, and target-angle semantics remain unchanged. The parent's learned representation is therefore semantically compatible with the same target-relative task, while transfer isolates the effect of adding radial support instead of confounding it with relearning from a fresh policy.", "strategy_link": "This directly advances the current Scientific strategy's highest-priority measured gap: test whether the baseline's 14-20 cm training support caused the lower-left reach blind spot. If the gap persists, the next direction will investigate angular or control asymmetry; if reach improves but hold stability degrades, hold stability becomes the next measured gap."}, "training_parent": "best_known"}, "reasoning": {"alternative": "The failures are caused by an angularly asymmetric reach/control or dynamics limitation rather than radial coverage; under this explanation, adding near-target radii will not remove the repeated lower-left failures, including failures at radii already covered by the baseline.", "contradicting_observation": "If the same lower-left episodes remain failures across both the newly covered 6-14 cm band and the already covered 14-20 cm band, especially with no improvement in first reach or hold duration, radial coverage is weakened and angular/control asymmetry becomes the preferred explanation. A new increase in positive-angle hold failures would also show a stability tradeoff rather than a clean resolution.", "evidence": [{"observation": "The current strategy identifies checkpoint-100352 as 97.0% on both 200-episode panels, with six repeatable failures concentrated at target angles about -122 to -145 degrees, and states that the baseline trained only on 14-20 cm radii.", "source": "research/postmortems.md"}, {"observation": "The six failed episodes include targets at 6.00, 8.37, and 12.99 cm as well as 15.09, 16.07, and 16.63 cm; five never reached tolerance and the remaining target held for only one step.", "source": "research/evaluations/e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2/evaluation-e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2-experiment-1-checkpoint-100352-200ep-seed0-d72ec900e7de.json"}, {"observation": "The training environment currently samples radii from (0.14, 0.20), while make_evaluation_env uses the protected official TARGET_RADIUS_RANGE; this intervention changes only the researcher-owned training range to (0.06, 0.20).", "source": "robot_learning/scenario/environment.py"}, {"observation": "The official task samples targets uniformly from 6-20 cm over the full angular range and requires a continuous 2-second hold, so the intervention preserves the official angular support and evaluation semantics.", "source": "research/scenario.md"}], "expected_observation": "On the same compatible research panel, failures at the newly covered 6-14 cm radii should decrease, with the repeated lower-left reach failures showing fewer no-reach outcomes and a higher overall success rate toward or above 98%. Improvement concentrated in the newly covered radial band supports the radial-coverage explanation; broader lower-left improvement would indicate that near-target training also improves the shared angular policy.", "initialization_reason": "Use transfer from best_known because the intervention changes only target sampling; the observation construction, action mapping, robot dynamics, reward, policy dimensions, and target-angle semantics remain unchanged. The parent's learned representation is therefore semantically compatible with the same target-relative task, while transfer isolates the effect of adding radial support instead of confounding it with relearning from a fresh policy.", "strategy_link": "This directly advances the current Scientific strategy's highest-priority measured gap: test whether the baseline's 14-20 cm training support caused the lower-left reach blind spot. If the gap persists, the next direction will investigate angular or control asymmetry; if reach improves but hold stability degrades, hold stability becomes the next measured gap."}, "recorded_at": "2026-09-08", "requested_evaluations": [{"candidate": "checkpoint-100352", "comparison_semantics": "9ed5c4c5e58a", "episodes": 200, "evaluation_semantics": "e7caaa2ce820", "instrument": "research_evaluation", "label": "experiment 2 checkpoint 100352", "metrics": {"comparison_semantics": "9ed5c4c5e58a", "episodes": 200, "evaluation_artifact": "research/evaluations/e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2/evaluation-e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2-experiment-2-checkpoint-100352-200ep-seed0-e7caaa2ce820.json", "evaluation_artifact_fingerprint": "5590c4330942a57af9dfb2f6f0191c1dafe21b1e6ea223e13a7b8db447fd08be", "evaluation_semantics": "e7caaa2ce820", "model_fingerprint": "487520e9073d283e99d06a6bedd3f7c5a7a5a7b8720ca5f0eec71fe600a121ad", "official_benchmark": false, "schema_version": 5, "seed": 0, "success_percent": 97.0}, "model_fingerprint": "487520e9073d283e99d06a6bedd3f7c5a7a5a7b8720ca5f0eec71fe600a121ad", "seed": 0}, {"candidate": "checkpoint-120832", "comparison_semantics": "9ed5c4c5e58a", "episodes": 200, "evaluation_semantics": "e7caaa2ce820", "instrument": "research_evaluation", "label": "experiment 2 checkpoint 120832", "metrics": {"comparison_semantics": "9ed5c4c5e58a", "episodes": 200, "evaluation_artifact": "research/evaluations/e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2/evaluation-e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2-experiment-2-checkpoint-120832-200ep-seed0-e7caaa2ce820.json", "evaluation_artifact_fingerprint": "600ea9562e6ee3f9ed003fb4d06515d5bd867a4984f21a3d34f21ce7fbdac219", "evaluation_semantics": "e7caaa2ce820", "model_fingerprint": "2e627d2bf3d2d7ec16bcebce6f8fa34468bf5f24ea4845c5f92b9c5b3ea6c864", "official_benchmark": false, "schema_version": 5, "seed": 0, "success_percent": 97.0}, "model_fingerprint": "2e627d2bf3d2d7ec16bcebce6f8fa34468bf5f24ea4845c5f92b9c5b3ea6c864", "seed": 0}], "schema_version": 1, "scientific_commit": "bd66b1bfc1dc3e482ce280609333597ada8b9716", "scientific_strategy": "## e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2 / Scientific strategy\n\n**Direction:** Improve the highest-priority measured gap of `checkpoint-100352`: failures on lower-left targets, concentrated at target angles from about -122 to -145 degrees. The next causal question is whether expanding training target coverage from the baseline's 14-20 cm radial range to the official 6-20 cm range removes this reach blind spot, rather than assuming that the training success proxy represents official behavior.\n\n**Lessons and limits:** The exact intervention was the fresh, unchanged PPO baseline trained for 120,000 steps. It established a near-threshold measured policy (97.0% on both 200-episode research panels at `checkpoint-100352`) but not the 98% objective; the final checkpoint measured 96.5%. This establishes a baseline and a candidate model, not a causal effect for any broader mechanism. The lower-left failure pattern is measured evidence, but the baseline alone does not distinguish radial coverage, angular asymmetry, dynamics, or optimization as its cause. The later training proxy peak is not used to override the measured policy behavior.\n\n**Open questions:** Whether the lower-left failures are caused by the training radial support, by angular coverage or representation, or by a control/dynamics limitation remains unresolved. The additional positive-angle failure at `checkpoint-120832` is a secondary hold-stability question: it may reflect late-training drift, but the available evidence does not make it the primary gap.\n\n**Conditional next steps:** Continue from `checkpoint-100352` with a training intervention that covers the official 6-20 cm radial range while preserving the full angular range. If lower-left reach failures persist, investigate angular or control asymmetry; if reach improves but positive-angle hold failures increase, make hold stability the next measured problem. Further development is higher value than terminal assessment while the measured policy remains below 98%.", "status": "closed", "task_reference_evaluations": [], "training_budget_steps": 120000, "training_parent": "best_known", "training_parent_lineage": {"artifact": "research/checkpoints/retained/e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2/e1-cada55eb5-c72b4f84c6f9a0022aba962ca964ba6627118b79a973b1e183e824694674491d", "candidate": "checkpoint-100352", "fingerprint": "c72b4f84c6f9a0022aba962ca964ba6627118b79a973b1e183e824694674491d", "identifier": "best_known", "origin_experiment": 1, "parameters": {"algorithm": {"name": "ppo"}, "policy": {"activation": "tanh", "net_arch": [64, 64]}, "ppo": {"batch_size": 64, "ent_coef": 0.01, "gae_lambda": 0.95, "gamma": 0.99, "learning_rate": 0.0003, "n_steps": 1024}, "training": {"checkpoint_every_steps": 5000, "n_envs": 1}}, "scientific_commit": "a2ee23673324b905733eacf14b37d733552803e1", "training_steps": 100352}, "training_seed": 0, "verdict": "researcher selected best_known as working", "working_lineage": {"artifact": "research/checkpoints/retained/e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2/e1-cada55eb5-c72b4f84c6f9a0022aba962ca964ba6627118b79a973b1e183e824694674491d", "candidate": "checkpoint-100352", "evaluation_artifacts": ["research/evaluations/e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2/evaluation-e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2-experiment-1-checkpoint-100352-200ep-seed0-d72ec900e7de.json", "research/evaluations/e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2/evaluation-e9b968c4-3137-4bd2-ac09-05422a64bcc2-experiment-1-checkpoint-100352-200ep-seed1-d72ec900e7de.json"], "fingerprint": "c72b4f84c6f9a0022aba962ca964ba6627118b79a973b1e183e824694674491d", "origin_experiment": 1, "parameters": {"algorithm": {"name": "ppo"}, "policy": {"activation": "tanh", "net_arch": [64, 64]}, "ppo": {"batch_size": 64, "ent_coef": 0.01, "gae_lambda": 0.95, "gamma": 0.99, "learning_rate": 0.0003, "n_steps": 1024}, "training": {"checkpoint_every_steps": 5000, "n_envs": 1}}, "reason": "The expanded 6-20 cm training-radius candidate achieved 97.0% on both measured checkpoints but retained the same six lower-left failures as the incumbent, including failures in both newly covered and already covered radial bands. Continue from the incumbent so the next experiment addresses the unresolved angular/control or dynamics explanation rather than extending an intervention that did not improve measured behavior.", "scientific_commit": "a2ee23673324b905733eacf14b37d733552803e1", "training_steps": 100352}}
